$Template:	V-wing Template ;; V-wing, fighter/interceptor with 2 primary banks
  +Use Template:                Rebel Fighter Template
  $Display Name:                V-wing Starfighter
  $Short name:                  V-wing
  $Species:                     Republic
    +Type:                     XSTR("Fighter", 813) ;
    +Maneuverability:          XSTR("Good", 814) ;;_4
    +Armor:                    XSTR("Moderate", 815) ;;_3
    +Manufacturer:             XSTR("Kuat Systems Engineering", 873)
    +Description:              XSTR(" ", 817)
    $end_multi_text
    +Tech Title:               XSTR("A-3 Nimbus V-wing Starfighter", 874)
    +Tech Description:         XSTR("The Alpha-3 Nimbus V-wing Starfighter was designed in accordance with a trend in Republic doctrine that sought to meet the Confederacy strength for strength: fielding fast, lightweight starfighters supported by capital ships.  Equipped with vertically-stacked ion engines tuned for high performance in the pitch axis, the V-wing was more than capable of dogfighting droid tri-fighters on equal terms--but its layer of light shielding gave it the advantage.  The craft made an excellent complement to the larger, slower ARC-170, while its two pairs of laser cannons gave it greater firepower, in the critical first seconds of a dogfight, than the Z-95 Headhunter (a fighter built more for endurance).

  The V-wing proved effective both during the Clone Wars and in the years following, during which the ship came to be one of the most important starfighters of the fledgling Empire.  Within a decade, though, innovations in compact starfighter components and weapon systems opened the door for other manufacturers to solicit new Imperial contracts.  As maintenance of the aging V-wing fleet grew more costly, a rival manufacturer, Sienar Fleet Systems, was able to persuade Imperial procurement to replace the V-wing with their newly designed Twin Ion Engine starfighter, which was not only more maneuverable but also far more cheaply mass-produced.  

  Though quickly phased out of the Imperial fleet, V-wings, now selling at a discount, began catching the attention of private buyers--no great surprise, given that this remained an agile shield-equipped fighter that was, by the standards of its price point, quite well-armed.  The largest sticking point was that the V-wing's stock configuration had no onboard hyperdrive; most buyers were forced to spend additional time and money installing modern compact hyperdrives.  Over time, upkeep costs have continued to rise and private usage has fallen, though the rare few V-wings that are well-maintained remain potent starfighters.", 875)
    $end_multi_text
    +Length:                   9 m
    +Gun Mounts:               2
    +Missile Banks:            1

