$Template:	Vulture Droid Template ;drone stlye that is mainly useful in large numbers
  +Use Template:                Common Fighter Template
  $Display Name:                Vulture Droid
  $Short name:                  VultureDroid
  $Species:                     Pirate
    +Type:                     XSTR("Fighter", 813)
    +Maneuverability:          XSTR("Good", 814) ;;_4
    +Armor:                    XSTR("Light", 822) ;;_2
    +Manufacturer:             XSTR("Haor Chall Engineering", 3005)
    +Description:              XSTR(" ", 817)
    $end_multi_text
    +Tech Title:               XSTR("Vulture-class Droid Starfighter", 3006)
    +Tech Description:
    XSTR("Today, there is no starfighter more common than the TIE/ln.  Twenty years ago, that crown belonged unquestionably to the Trade Federation Vulture droid.

  The Vulture-class--or, more formally, the Variable Geometry Self-Propelled Battle Droid--was first designed and built in the cathedral-factories of the insectoid Xi Char, whose religion saw precision manufacturing as a sacred endeavor.  The craft served the Trade Federation during the blockade and invasion of Naboo, and when the Federation licensed its armies to the Confederacy of Independent Systems ten years later, the Vulture became the Confederacy's primary space superiority fighter, churned out by Separatist factories in swarms of tens of millions.

  Small, fast, and maneuverable, the Vulture suffers from light armament and a lack of shields.  By far its most consequential weakness, however, is found in the control system.  The earliest Vulture droids were slaved to a central computer aboard a droid control ship, but the Battle of Naboo painfully demonstrated what a fatal flaw that could be.  By the time of the Clone Wars, most Separatist droids, Vultures included, carried integrated droid brains.  Mass-produced droid brains struggle to keep up with their organic counterparts, and CIS commanders tended to find their Vulture squadrons tactically sluggish, easily confused, and incapable of following complex orders.  All parties agreed, though, that this was a state of affairs far preferable to the vulnerability of a central computer.

  But history, they say, rhymes like poetry, and at the Clone Wars' close the countless Vulture droids across the Galaxy were once more abruptly shut down by a central signal--this time sent by the Republic operatives who had located and neutralized the Separatist Council.

  The hard shutoff signal did considerable damage to the software of most of the droids which received it, and many of the millions of Vultures that collected in scrapyards after the war were simply stripped down for parts.  But getting a droid operational again was no great challenge for a determined tech, and not a few criminal groups, private organizations, and small governments--especially those with manpower difficulties--have elected to field squadrons of refurbished Vultures in patrol and escort roles.  While the Vulture has fallen far from its wartime ubiquity, it is not an uncommon sight, especially in the Outer Rim.

  This configuration, an original Mark 1 upgraded with a droid brain, carries four wing-mounted blaster cannons and a pair of light ordnance launchers.", 3007)
    $end_multi_text
    +Length:                   6.9 m
    +Gun Mounts:               1
    +Missile Banks:            1 

