;; ========================================================================
;;                   			  Venator
$Template:	Venator #Rep Template
  +Use Template:                Common CruiserLarge Template
  $Display Name:                Venator Cruiser
  $Short name:                  Venator (Rep)
  $Species:                     Republic
  +Tech Title:                  XSTR("Venator-class Cruiser", 968)
  +Tech Description:
  XSTR("Once the signature ship of the line for the Republic Navy, the Venator-class Cruiser is now a casualty of the massive changes that have swept through the Galaxy over the past two decades.  Contracted by the Republic to build a line combatant with a heavy focus on starfighter deployment, Kuat Drive Yards designed and constructed the Venator in relative secrecy, both to limit possible espionage and because this was their first foray into constructing large capital ships.  This veil of secrecy was lifted abruptly not long after the sudden onset of the Clone Wars, when Venators were rapidly deployed from the Core all the way to the Outer Rim.

  Seeing immediate and sustained combat, Kuat's design proved successful, and the vessel remained the backbone of the Republic fleet throughout the war.  Following the defeat of the Separatists, Venators briefly retained their role as the Empire's primary line combatant, and they were beginning to undergo substantial retrofits as the Imperial Navy took form.  However, subsequent rounds of technological advancement and doctrine reassessment--informed by the many lessons learned in the Clone Wars--eventually persuaded the Starfleet to scrap and completely replace the Venator in favor of Kuat's new Imperial Star Destroyer line.  The vast number of Venators ordered to the scrapyards, even in good condition, resulted in a massive underground network of particularly powerful private organizations acquiring components and even entire nearly intact vessels.
  
  Such a development was hardly surprising, as the Venator, scarcely two decades old at the onset of the Galactic Civil War, is still highly competitive--especially with hangars full of modern fighters.  But its primary armament remains potent as well.  Originally optimized for firing broadsides from battle lines, its massive batteries are commonly refitted by well-funded buyers with top-of-the-line aftermarket turbolasers.  The vast Venator scrapyards also ensure that black-market buyers can dip into a massive supply of replacement components, though it is not uncommon for deals in such a high-end market to turn fatal.  The end result is that many privately-modified cruisers far outclass their original specifications in armor, armament, and power output ratings.

  Perhaps the Venator's most appealing aspect is its core design as a carrier, which is especially vital for nomadic factions that require hangar space for dozens of fighters and transports.  The ability to deploy fighter screens rapidly via both the massive central aperture and the side hangars is critical for providing protection from hostile forces, as even modified Venators are vulnerable to attacks from modern heavy starfighters.  Cut-up and bolted-together as they may be, the many Venators active throughout the fleets of criminals and local governments alike make it very clear that while the Empire may have forgotten the Venator, the rest of the Galaxy has done anything but.", 969)
  $end_multi_text
